      "id": 33,
      "title": "DM Draft: The Settling of the Church of Aelith",
      "type": "Organization",
      "categoryId": 4,
      "summary": "The only church founded because of a question rather than a crisis: after sixty years of deliberation, the elves agreed in the year 214 that the counted, paper-keeping world required them to have a priesthood, and then planted their first temple and waited a century for the roof.",
      "body": "Every other church on Libris settled because something broke. Aelith's settled because something was asked. The elves had kept their faith for two centuries of the count and all of [[The Uncounted Time]] before it without a single priest, temple, or written word of doctrine, and the Church of Aelith exists, in the end, because the shorter-lived peoples would not stop asking the elves questions and writing down the answers wrong.\n\n## Before the church\n\nElven faith did not need keeping, because elves are the keeping. Aelith shaped her people for the long ripening: a life is a draft, revised across centuries toward the finished self it becomes in [[Aethelis, the Unchanging|Aethelis]], and death is not the interruption of that work but its final season. A people whose elders remember the making of things do not need archives, and a people whose borders are suggested rather than fenced does not need missions. Bards carried the teaching as living archives. Families carried it the rest of the way. It worked, the way everything elven works, for longer than most nations have existed.\n\nWhat it could not survive was an audience. The counted world ran on paper. Dwarven clerks compiling their records asked where elven dead go, received four answers from four elders, each correct and each phrased differently, and entered all four as four different realms. Human kings asked whether the elven court recognized this treaty or that power, and got replies in the third generation, from the grandchildren of those who had been asked. The elves were not being evasive. They were being thorough, at elven speed, in a world that had begun keeping ledgers, and the mismatch produced a century of small, compounding errors in everybody else's books.\n\n## The settling\n\nThe church dates its settling to the year 214 of the First Age, which is the year the Long Deliberation ended. The Deliberation had begun, by the church's own count, sixty years earlier, when the elven court first took up the question of whether the elves required a priesthood. It is the only founding on record that took longer than the founding generation of some peoples, and three of the elves who opened the question died while it was being considered, which the church does not regard as an objection to the process. Their successors signed the conclusion in their places, in their names, as a matter of course: the dead had been ripening toward the same answer.\n\nThe conclusion was yes, narrowly defined. The church would exist to do the things memory alone could not: speak for the faith to peoples who keep records, at speeds records require; keep the teaching one teaching across a realm with no internal borders; and conduct the finishing of elves who die among strangers, far from any elder who knows their draft.\n\nIts first temple was planted, not built: a grove set out in the year 214, arranged as a hall, with the understanding that it would be usable in about a century. The church held its services in the open field beside it in the meantime, and then under young trees, and then under a green roof, and the grove is a temple now, and has been for some time. Visitors who ask why the oldest church building of the elves looks like a forest are told that it is a forest, and that this was the point, and that they may sit down.\n\n## What they took charge of\n\nThe finishing, first: Aelith's priests conduct the rites that close a life's draft and ferry the dead to Aethelis, where the ripening completes. They are blunt, in their gentle way, about the alternative: a soul sent to [[Khavora, the Maelstrom Womb|Khavora]] has been harvested early, and a finished thing set down in that realm is a cruelty, like a bound book left in a river. See [[Patrons]].\n\nThe drafts, second. The church keeps, for every elf who asks it, a life-draft: a record begun in youth and revised at intervals across the centuries, not of deeds but of becomings, what the self was, what it is revising toward, what remains rough. At death the draft is read once, corrected by those who knew the dead best, and put away as final. It is the only archive the church keeps, and pointedly so: deeds belong to other peoples' ledgers, but a becoming can only be recorded by the people who watched it happen.\n\nAnd the attesting, third, which the shorter-lived peoples value most. An elven elder's memory is a primary source for events everyone else calls myth, and the church certifies such memories for foreign courts, historians, and treaty disputes: an elder speaks, the church examines, and what survives examination enters the record with the church's seal on it. Kings have rebuilt their histories on one afternoon of attested elven memory. The dwarves charge less for paper and are used for everything else: exasperating, and secretly relied on.\n\n## An answer, when it ripens\n\nThe church's oddity is famous enough to be a proverb in three languages: it answers slowly. A petition to the Church of Aelith receives its reply in years, sometimes in decades, and the reply is never hurried by war, famine, or the petitioner's entirely reasonable screaming. This is not laziness. It is the whole theology applied to correspondence: an answer given before the question has ripened is a guess, and the church does not guess about souls.\n\nWhat outsiders discover, usually too late to help their tempers, is the other half of the proverb: an elven answer never needs a second letter. The reply, when it comes, is final, complete, and correct in ways that only become apparent later. The Conclave, whose charter runs on written claims and dwarven dates, keeps a standing clerk assigned to elven correspondence, whose duties are to file the questions and wait. See [[The Founding of the Conclave]]. Three holders of that post have retired and been replaced before certain answers arrived. The church sent each of them a courteous note of thanks, in due course.\n\n## The church today\n\nIt remains the smallest priesthood of any major church relative to its people, and the most confident. Aelith's is the one church that has never revised a doctrine, sent a missionary, or hurried, and its priests regard the younger churches the way their people regard humans: with genuine affection, and the settled knowledge that brilliance and brevity are the same thing seen from far away. Other churches settled around books, roads, and schools. See [[The Settling of the Church of Vharess]]. Aelith's settled around a decision it took sixty years to make, and planted its temple, and waited for the roof.\n\nThe next church belonged to a people for whom waiting is a discipline of an entirely different kind. See [[The Settling of the Church of Urgath]].",
      "visibility": "draft",
      "tags": [
        "history",
        "aelith",
        "church",
        "elves",
        "ripening",
        "patience"
      ],
      "pinned": false,
