      "id": 36,
      "title": "DM Draft: The Settling of the Church of Sevara",
      "type": "Organization",
      "categoryId": 4,
      "summary": "The late church: tieflings surface in other peoples' records around the year 860, already formed, and Sevara's church is recognized in 911 when a port court rules that priests who bury the unclaimed cannot be denied standing for doing it. Its temples are shelters with doors that do not lock, and its largest congregation is other peoples' dead.",
      "body": "Every other church in this series was settled by its own people, in its own country, around its own altars. Sevara's was settled in other peoples' cities, in their records, in their margins, by a people with no country at all. It is the youngest of the great churches by centuries, unless it is not, and the argument about which is the most tiefling thing about it.\n\n## Before the church\n\nThe first problem is that the record starts late. Other peoples' archives, which between them cover the count fairly well, do not mention tieflings at all until around the year 860 of the First Age, when the name begins appearing in port toll lists, a census margin, a court record about a horned dockworker whose testimony a judge accepted and then apologized for accepting. After 860 the references multiply quickly, and they describe a people already formed: diaspora, customs, temples of a sort, a Patron who answers. Scholars have argued for centuries about what the silence before 860 means. Sevara may have shaped her people last of the eight, assembling them from what the other Patrons left unclaimed, late in the world's making and later still in its records. Or the tieflings were there for ages and simply beneath the record's attention, the way the poor of any age are. See [[The Uncounted Time]].\n\nThe church itself holds a third position, cheerfully: the asking started before the records did, and the records are not the faith's problem.\n\nWhat is certain is that Sevara's work is older than her temples. Priests of other churches had recorded the phenomenon for generations before anyone attached a name to it: the begging dead at the edges of the two realms, the strays, the unclaimed, the ones nobody was coming for, going somewhere. Not up the expected roads. Answered. The other churches found this irregular and faintly improper, like a door in a wall they had agreed was solid.\n\n## The settling\n\nThe church dates its settling to the year 911, and like Fennik's, the date comes from a form, but where the gnomes treated theirs as a joke, the tieflings treat theirs as a trophy. See [[The Settling of the Church of Fennik]].\n\nIn 911 a wealthy human merchant died in a great port city belonging to no church, as a great many humans do, and left his estate in trust with a will whose whole instruction was that he be buried by whoever would have him. The city's churches, each of which had declined his membership in life over one irregularity or another, discovered a lively interest in him in death, and the dispute over the estate went before the city court with three priesthoods arguing that the unclaimed dead were theirs by custom. Sevara's shelter-priests buried him during the first week of the argument, having asked him, and being, as they said in court, the only party who had.\n\nThe ruling, which lawyers still cite, held that a body of priests who bury, shelter, and answer could not be denied standing as a church merely because the older churches found their Patron's methods unseemly. The Church of Sevara was entered in the registry of churches that year, last on the list, and its representatives noted for the record that last is where they do their best work. A little over a century later it signed the Conclave's charter, again last, and the clerk ruled blank lines beneath its name. See [[The Founding of the Conclave]].\n\n## What they took charge of\n\nThe unclaimed, in all their varieties. Sevara's temples double as shelters, orphanages, and banks of favors, and her priests bury the dead nobody else will take: the churchless, the exiled, the unknown washed up by the tide of a port. Her own people's dead choose their realm by asking, the only dead on Libris who do, and her priests keep both roads open and charge nothing for the asking. See [[Patrons]]. But the church's largest congregation, by its own cheerful accounting, is other peoples' strays, and first among them the human dead, who have no ferry of their own and arrive at Sevara's doors in numbers no other church has to plan for.\n\nThe asking, as an institution: anyone may come to a Sevara temple with a need, state it plainly, and be answered or honestly told why not. The paladins of the church swear it as the Answering Oath, to come when called by anyone with no one else to call, which makes them the most welcome knights on the continent and the most overworked. And the carrying of petitions: because the church takes the people nobody else represents, it became, in time, the voice of the churchless before the law, and carries most human petitions to the Conclave to this day.\n\n## Doors that do not lock\n\nThe oddity every traveler notices is that a temple of Sevara is built without locks. The doors stand open at all hours, in all weathers, in the worst quarters of the worst ports, because the church's founding fact is that the call can come at any hour and from anyone, and a locked door would be a doctrine it does not hold. What the temples own is held accordingly: very little that cannot be replaced, and a great deal that is not property at all, since the church's real treasury is favors, recorded nowhere and remembered perfectly by a diaspora that can find its own kind in any port within a day.\n\nOutsiders, watching the church grow fat on every disaster and every unclaimed death, mutter from time to time that Sevara does suspiciously well out of things falling apart, and darker versions of the mutter exist. The church's reply is on record and is always the same: somebody's church had to be the one at the bottom of the hill. They invite the mutterers to come and help carry.\n\n## The church today\n\nIt is the fastest-growing of the churches and the least respectable, two facts it insists are related. Its temples anchor tiefling quarters in every port, its priests are the continent's undertakers of last resort, and its paladins are a standing argument that the bottom of every list is a load-bearing position. There is one people left in this series with no settling to record, because there was never a church to settle. Their entry is not a founding. It is the shape of the absence the other eleven keep leaving behind. See [[The Borrowed Faiths of Humans]].",
      "visibility": "draft",
