      "id": 41,
      "title": "DM Draft: The Settling of the Church of Water",
      "type": "Organization",
      "categoryId": 4,
      "summary": "Water's church settled along a line rather than in a place: in the year 275 a widowed ferry keeper traveled her river source to sea in one season, leaving a chart and a sworn keeper at every landing. The chain she founded still keeps the charts, the ferries, and the registers of what the water gives back.",
      "body": "Of all the faiths in this series, [[Water]]'s is the only one that could not have been settled in a place, because its shard is not in places. Libris lasts by staying. Water lasts by going, and its church, when it finally formed, formed along a line: the whole length of a river, walked and rowed and charted by one woman in one season, with a keeper left standing at every landing behind her.\n\n## Before the church\n\nWater's people were keeping its customs wherever water was worked, and they were not one people. Sailors kept the sea's etiquette and the proverbs that go with it. Ferry keepers kept their crossings, their fares, and their private knowledge of how the channel moved in flood season. Well-diggers kept the soundings of their districts in memory and took the locations of the reliable drought wells to their graves as a kind of inheritance. Millers kept the river's temper in their bones. Every landing, port, and well-field had the faith in pieces, and the pieces did not travel, which was the problem the faith of the journey had somehow failed to solve for itself.\n\nA chart that ends at your own headland is a rumor about the rest of the river. Boats were lost in sight of shores whose pilots knew the water perfectly and had never written it down, wells failed in droughts two valleys from wells that held, and the church to be had all the knowledge it would ever need, distributed among ten thousand keepers like rain over a watershed, none of it gathered anywhere.\n\n## The settling\n\nIn the year 275 of the First Age, a ferry keeper of the great river, widowed that spring when the flood took her husband at their own crossing, did the thing the church now counts as its founding. She left the ferry to her brother's family, took a boat at the river's source in the melt season, and traveled the whole of it, source to sea, in one journey, stopping at every landing, sounding every channel, and leaving behind at each one two things: a copy of her growing chart, and a keeper sworn to maintain it, add to it, and share it with the next boat down.\n\nShe reached the sea in the autumn and, by the accounts kept at the landings, added the last sounding herself and then took service on a sea boat, having apparently decided the journey was the point. The church of Water dates its settling from her Descent, and calls her, in the absence of any name the charts agree on, the First Ferryman, a title her own sex and trade make cheerful nonsense of, which the church regards as correct in every way that matters.\n\nThe chain she left behind is the church's skeleton to this day: a line of keepers, each holding the chart for their reach of the water, each bound to pass copies up and down the line, so that the whole river, and in time every navigable river and coast the faith serves, exists on paper somewhere, changing as the water changes, in handwriting that never dries.\n\n## What they took charge of\n\nThe charts and the marks: channels sounded, shoals marked, crossings rated by season, wells registered with their depths and their drought behavior, all of it kept current by the keepers and available to any traveler for the asking. The chart is the church's scripture and its charity in one object, and a keeper who falsifies a sounding is the faith's one unforgivable figure, worse than a priest of Urgath who rigs a trial, because people die of it in the dark, in the rain, trusting paper.\n\nThe ferries: licensed crossings on every served river, with fares set by custom and one rule above the fares, which is that a ferry does not refuse a crossing in extremity for want of payment. The church is aware of the irony and preaches it plainly: we ferry the living. The dead have their own ferrymen, and Water's priests bury drowned strangers with the same care as the charts, but the road after is not theirs to row. No Element faith ferries souls. See [[The Settling of the Church of Fire]].\n\nAnd the Returning: the sea gives back what it took, years later and miles away, worn smooth and changed, and the church keeps the shore registers of what the water returns, wreckage, cargo, belongings, and the dead, and carries word of it to the families along the keeper chain. Sailors swear that water remembers. The church takes no position on the swearing. It keeps the registers, which come to the same thing, in handwriting.\n\n## Priests who travel\n\nThe church's oddity fits its shard: it has almost no settled clergy. Its priests are keepers and travelers, its temples are ferry houses and well houses and the chart rooms of ports, and its central rite is the Descent, which every new priest makes once, alone, along their own home water from source to sea or spring to shore, charting as they go. The chart a priest draws on that journey is examined at the end of it by the keepers at the sea, corrected in their hands without ceremony, and added to the chain's copies. A priest is thereafter addressed by the name of their water, which outsiders find poetic and the church finds merely accurate, since ask the river the way, but do not ask it twice: it will not give the same answer, and neither, after the Descent, will the priest.\n\nIts prayers are practical, for a safe crossing, a fair current, a spring that holds, and its blessings arrive in the shape its faithful expect from a living shard: exactly enough, exactly when needed, and never promised twice.\n\n## The church today\n\nIt is the faith of every working shore, a network more than an institution, strongest wherever people make their living on the journey rather than the place. Its feud with the church of [[Libris]] continues at the pace of geology, ground against going, and its keepers and the ground-priests meet at every harbor wall and gorge bridge in the world to conduct it with great professional courtesy. See [[The Settling of the Church of Libris]]. Against Fire its contention is total, unmake against unmake. With [[Air]] it is partner as often as rival, rain their joint work and the storm their argument, and Air's is the last of the Element faiths in this series. See [[The Settling of the Church of Air]].",
