      "id": 42,
      "title": "DM Draft: The Settling of the Church of Air",
      "type": "Organization",
      "categoryId": 4,
      "summary": "Air's faith was everywhere and unorganized for centuries because there was nothing visible to gather around. It settled in the year 390, the summer the wind stopped, when sky readers, millers, and singers organized the response among themselves and never disbanded.",
      "body": "Of all the faiths in this series, [[Air]]'s took the longest to settle and left the least to show for it, which the church considers two ways of saying the same thing. Its shard is the one nobody has ever seen. A faith cannot gather around an altar nobody can point at, and Air's followers spent the first centuries of the counted age as the opposite of a church: everywhere, devout, and completely unorganized, like their element.\n\n## Before the church\n\nEveryone kept Air's customs because everyone breathed, and the trades that worked the wind kept them formally. Shepherds read the sky from the high pastures and prayed with their faces to it, so it did not have to turn around to hear them. Millers kept the mill customs and the calendar of winds. Singers held that the shard that carries every song deserves a tithe of them. Bird keepers, sail wrights, and the midwives who listened for the first cry and the physicians who listened for the last kept the faith's edges, the beginnings and endings of the loan.\n\nNone of it was a church, and attempts to make it one kept dissolving, for a reason the faithful state without embarrassment: there was nothing to organize around. Water had crossings, Fire had hearths, [[Libris]] had ground, all of them places where a body could be set in charge. Air had weathers, which do not keep office hours, and a loan every living thing held personally, whether or not any priest stood near. Twice in the early centuries, by the other churches' records, gatherings tried to settle the faith and adjourned having settled nothing but a date to try again.\n\n## The settling\n\nIn the year 390 of the First Age came the summer the wind stopped. It is the worst recorded calm in the counted age: a season of still air lying over the mill country and the sea lanes together, sails slack within sight of home ports, mill sails still above full granaries, smoke hanging over towns in a gray sheet that did not lift for weeks, and in the deep mines the quiet killer at its work, air that stops moving and takes men without malice and without witnesses. Thousands sat idle, hungry in the middle of harvest, learning the lesson Air teaches by absence: the loan settles in minutes, and everything mortals do rides on it.\n\nThe faith settled out of the response, which the trades organized among themselves when no one else did. The shepherds' sky readers took stations on the headlands and high pastures and began the Watch, reading the wind's return in signs and sending word down by bird and bell faster than the calm could be complained about. The millers scheduled the region's grinding against the returning breezes cooperatively, so a little wind fed everyone in turn. The singers, in the faith's own account of itself, kept the towns breathing, carrying songs, news, and tempers from street to street through the still weeks, on the theory that a people still singing had not yet suffocated.\n\nWhen the wind came back, in the autumn, all at once and at gale strength, the arrangement did not disband. It had become, almost without anyone voting on it, a body that answered for something: the reading of the sky, the carrying of messages, and the attendance of breath. The church of Air dates its settling from the summer it was needed, and notes that it was founded, uniquely among the faiths, during the one season its shard appeared to be doing nothing at all.\n\n## What they took charge of\n\nThe Watch first: stations on headlands, passes, and high pastures, kept by sky readers who record the wind by the hour and send weather word across the country by bird, bell, and signal fire, a service every other faith and most governments quietly depend on and the church provides to all askers, since weather is not improved by being kept secret. A blessing from Air is a fair wind that holds, three dry days for the harvest, breath coming easy in a sickroom, and the Watch is the church doing systematically for whole countries what the shard does personally for the faithful: answering in signs, quickly, without ceremony.\n\nSecond, the carrying of messages, which the church holds to be a sacred extension of its shard's oldest work. Every prayer ever offered to any power in creation was carried on Air's loan. The other shards receive worship. Air delivers it. The church's bird lines and bell codes and sung relays are that doctrine with feathers on, and its messengers take a corresponding oath: to carry faithfully what they are given, unopened and unaltered, and never to wonder too hard, on duty, about the difference between a message and a prayer.\n\nThird, the two breaths. Air's priests attend the beginnings and endings of the loan: they are called to births, to listen for the first cry and witness the account opened, and to deathbeds, to witness the last breath returned, and their attendance at those thresholds is the faith's most requested service and its most misunderstood. They do not ferry the dead, as no Element faith does. They witness the return of what was borrowed, and certify it, and leave the road to those whose charge it is, and families who have heard a priest say the breath is returned, all of it, in full, describe the sentence as the most comfort anyone offered them.\n\n## The unwritten liturgy\n\nThe church's oddity is its archive: there is hardly one. Air's faith keeps no written liturgy, on the stated ground that writing belongs to the ground's way of remembering, and its prayers, wind calendars, and signal codes are carried in trained memory and sung. Its singers are therefore not decoration but infrastructure, ordained, sworn, and examined like any other clergy, and the church's whole memory fits in the heads of its people, which the faithful call the safest archive there is, since it cannot burn, and its critics call a bonfire waiting for a plague year, which the church, being Air's, answers by spreading copies: every priest teaches the whole liturgy to two others, always, as a matter of doctrine, the way breath itself is distributed.\n\n## The church today\n\nIt has the smallest footprint of any faith in this series: wall-less roofed pavilions on high ground, which cannot be shut because their shard cannot be shut out, Watch towers, bird lofts, and the open-air shrines of the high pastures. Its priests are the sky's clerks and the wind's witnesses, called on by everyone and owned by no one, and its last charges in this series are the two that are not churches proper at all: [[Time]]'s keepers, who keep the count of the largest shard of all, and the little shrines of the [[Nature Spirits]], the least of the shards, which Air's restless own already haunt. See [[Time's Keepers]] and [[The Shrines of the Nature Spirits]].",
      "visibility": "draft",
      "tags": [
        "history",
        "air",
        "church",
        "element",
        "wind",
        "breath",
        "messages"
      ],
      "pinned": false,
      "parentId": 47,
      "relations": []
