      "id": 44,
      "title": "DM Draft: The Shrines of the Nature Spirits",
      "type": "Organization",
      "categoryId": 4,
      "summary": "The Nature Spirits have no church and no founding, because there was never a moment when they were not already being kept. A shrine is a place, a spirit, and a keeper, usually the family whose land borders the ground, and it lasts exactly as long as the keeping does.",
      "body": "This series ends where religion on Libris begins: underneath all the churches, older than every one of them, with the least of the shards. The [[Nature Spirits]] have no church, and they also have no founding, because there was never a moment when they were not already being kept. Every other entry in this series describes something mortals built around a shard. The shrines describe what was already there when the building started.\n\n## Older than the count\n\nA spirit is the power behind a single place, this mountain, this river, this grove, and it was there before the peoples were. The [[Patrons]] gathered souls and formed the races. The Elements stood behind the world's foundations. The spirits simply occupied their ground, unrecorded and unbothered, and mortal custom around them has the flavor it has had ever since: negotiation between neighbors. Leave the first fish of the catch. Do not cut the grove's heart trees. Ask the bridge spirit's leave before rebuilding the bridge. Tell the mountain when you are bringing strangers over the pass.\n\nNone of that was ever founded, voted on, or written down at the time, and none of it belongs to the counted age, though it fills the counted age from one end to the other. The shrines are the one part of this series the keepers of [[Time]] cannot date, and the only one they do not try to. A shrine's history is not a chronicle. It is a stack of arrangements, renewed family by family, offense by offense, gift by gift, in a place that remembers individuals, keeps grudges by name, and has favorites no village would have picked.\n\n## What a shrine is\n\nThere is no standard form, because there is no standard spirit. A shrine may be a cairn at a pass, a carved post at a ford, a basin kept clean at a spring, a stone left unplowed in the middle of a field, or a particular tree with a fence around it and a bench for offerings. What makes it a shrine is not its shape but its staffing: a keeper, which is to say someone local who maintains the place, remembers its customs, and tells them to strangers. Most often the keepership rides with the land, held by the family whose farm borders the ground and handed down with the deed, and the keeper's duties are the faith's whole liturgy, in miniature. Keep the place. Know the rules. Warn the traveler. And when someone offends, as someone eventually does, arrange the making-right, which is conducted at the place itself, in replacement, replanting, labor, or apology, according to what the ground is owed.\n\nTwo limits define the whole institution, if a thousand unrelated customs can be called one. A shrine cannot be founded by an institution: a church or a crown can build a handsome monument to a spirit, and spirits are not known to object to masonry, but the monument is not a shrine until someone whose life touches the ground keeps it as a neighbor, and not as a policy. And a shrine lapses when its keeping lapses. If the keeper's line dies out or moves away and nobody takes the duty up, the spirit remains in its ground, with its opinions, and the customs that made those opinions safe to live beside are forgotten in a generation. Lapsed shrines are a recognized hazard of the roads: ground under a power whose rules nobody alive remembers, where the traveler's rule is the only liturgy left. Every valley has its own small power and its own opinion. Ask before you act on either.\n\n## Among the churches\n\nThe great churches, to their credit, mostly leave the shrines alone, and their priests on the road observe local custom like anyone else, since a spirit's hold inside its own ground is close to total and indifferent to theology. The ground-priests of [[Libris]] are the shrines' most natural colleagues, the mountain spirits being largely their shard's kin, and Water's keepers likewise along the rivers, and the formal courtesies run both ways: a shrine's keeper will warn the village when the mountain is restless in its weather, and a church raising a wall on a spirit's ground sends to ask the customs first. The frictions are real but small and constant, a parish boundary drawn through a grove, a worked place, a bridge, a battlefield, a graveyard, gathering a spirit of its own beneath a faith that thought the ground was already spoken for, and they are settled the way everything at this scale is settled: locally, by negotiation, by people who will all still be there afterward.\n\n## The shrines today\n\nThey are beyond counting, which is the point. No census of them exists and none could: new ones gather wherever mortal doing soaks deep enough into a place, and old ones lapse wherever the keeping fails, and both happen every year without reference to any authority in this series. A spirit answers constantly and locally, in the coin of its own place: the pass clear a day early, the lost child found asleep in the grove and warm, the flood that took the fence and spared the house. It is the smallest answering there is, and the most personal, and for most mortals, on most days, it is the only one that matters.\n\nThe churches in this series were built, one by one, around shards too large to meet. The shrines were never built at all. They accumulated, the way paths do, where feet and favors actually went, and they will outlast the churches for the same reason paths outlast palaces: they are not maintained by institutions. They are maintained by neighbors, which is the world's oldest arrangement, and, all the way down, still its commonest one.",
